IMPORT prelude

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992, 1992 c. 4 -- part ssc5 (Parts VI to X).
-- HELPERS, NOT PROVISIONS: three-valued logic over MAYBE BOOLEAN, so that a NOTHING from another
-- provision (a seam, or a rule that points outside the text) flows through and is never turned into FALSE.
-- Names begin with the label of the first provision of this part that needs them (s 124(1)), as the
-- naming rule requires; no source text is quoted here.
--   both hold:       JUST FALSE if either is JUST FALSE; JUST TRUE if both are JUST TRUE; otherwise NOTHING
--   either holds:    JUST TRUE if either is JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE if both are JUST FALSE; otherwise NOTHING
--   does not hold:   JUST TRUE <-> JUST FALSE; NOTHING stays NOTHING

GIVEN `the first answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the second answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) both hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the first answer`
    WHEN JUST x THEN
        IF x
        THEN `the second answer`
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER `the second answer`
        WHEN JUST y THEN
            IF y
            THEN NOTHING
            ELSE JUST FALSE
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

GIVEN `the first answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the second answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) either holds` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the first answer`
    WHEN JUST x THEN
        IF x
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE `the second answer`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        CONSIDER `the second answer`
        WHEN JUST y THEN
            IF y
            THEN JUST TRUE
            ELSE NOTHING
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

GIVEN `the answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) does not hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the answer`
    WHEN JUST x THEN
        IF x
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- TRUE only when the answer is JUST TRUE (NOTHING and JUST FALSE both give FALSE).
GIVEN `the answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) is known to hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the answer`
    WHEN JUST x THEN x
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

-- A relative-clause grant (rule P2): JUST TRUE when the answer is JUST TRUE; anything else is NOTHING.
GIVEN `the answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) holds or else nothing` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the answer`
    WHEN JUST x THEN
        IF x
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- A known fact becomes an answer.
GIVEN `the fact` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) the fact as an answer` MEANS JUST `the fact`

-- A table of definitions: how an Act defines a term, and what it points at.  Shared by the definition
-- tables of ss 122(1), 137(1), 138(5), 147(1) and 150(2); it is declared once, here, under the label of the
-- first of them.
DECLARE `s 122(1) Way of defining` IS ONE OF
    `s 122(1) a definition by "means"`
    `s 122(1) a definition by "includes"`
    `s 122(1) a construction by reference to another provision`
    `s 122(1) a meaning borrowed from another Act or instrument`
    `s 122(1) a plain statement of what the term is`
    `s 122(1) blank in this text`

DECLARE `s 122(1) A definition` HAS
    `the way it is defined`      IS A `s 122(1) Way of defining`
    `what it points at or says`  IS A STRING

-- A conjunction of any number of answers (the "if" of a list of conditions): JUST FALSE if any is JUST FALSE,
-- otherwise NOTHING if any is NOTHING, otherwise JUST TRUE; the empty list is JUST TRUE.
GIVEN answers IS A LIST OF MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 124(1) all of these hold` MEANS
    CONSIDER answers
    WHEN EMPTY THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN first FOLLOWED BY rest THEN `s 124(1) both hold` first (`s 124(1) all of these hold` rest)
